============================================================
PERFORMANCE (3-way, 2x15 interleaved; two complete runs)
============================================================
Run 1:
  accepted          0.267488 ms
  original pipeline 0.258272 ms   (-3.45 % vs accepted)
  lb6 pipeline      0.259344 ms   (-3.04 % vs accepted, +0.42 % vs original)
Run 2 (accepted polluted by 0.564 outlier):
  accepted          0.280192 ms
  original pipeline 0.261200 ms   (-6.78 % vs accepted)
  lb6 pipeline      0.268272 ms   (-4.25 % vs accepted, +2.71 % vs original)

Consolidated:
  lb6 vs accepted : -3.04 %, -4.25 %  (consistently faster, meets >1% gate)
  lb6 vs original : +0.42 %, +2.71 %  (consistently SLOWER than 47-reg original)

============================================================
VERDICT: REJECT
============================================================
launch_bounds is a working lever for the RESOURCE axis (40 regs, 6 blocks, 80%
occupancy, zero spills) but it is NOT the bottleneck: the recovered occupancy does
not translate to wall-clock. The unconstrained 47-register pipeline encodes more
ILP per iteration and remains ~0.4-2.7% faster than the lb6 form across all runs.
So the register-pressure-recovery-via-launch_bounds tuning route is rejected as an
improvement. (Separately noted: BOTH pipelined forms are ~3-7% faster than accepted
against fresh same-state accepted, but that pipeline-vs-accepted question belongs to
the prior experiment's verdict, not this tuning experiment.)

